
  A fixed interpreter inside the core maps descriptor keys to checks.

  [3mTradeoffs:[0m the schema is pure JSON, so the same object could later drive server-side validation or be stored — real
  value if that's on the horizon. But every new rule type means editing the central interpreter, and custom one-off
  rules don't fit without an escape hatch that reintroduces functions anyway. Complexity: trivial-to-moderate.

  [1mC. Native HTML5 constraint validation[0m

  Put [38;5;153mrequired[39m, [38;5;153mminlength[39m, [38;5;153mtype="email"[39m, [38;5;153mpattern[39m on the inputs and use the browser's Constraint Validation API; the
  shared JS is a thin layer for custom messages and consistent rendering.

  [3mTradeoffs:[0m by far the least code, and you get accessibility and mobile keyboards free. But the rules then live in each
  form's markup, so "reusable across forms" degrades to a copy-paste convention — which is the problem you asked me to
  solve. Also awkward to unit-test in Node without a DOM. Complexity: trivial.

  [1mWhy A.[0m You said several forms with presence plus format/length rules, and no cross-field or async. A is the only
  option where a new rule type costs one exported function and a unit test, and where the rules stay testable without a
  browser. B only pays off if the schema needs to cross a process boundary, which nothing in the current code suggests —
  [38;5;153mlogin()[39m is a stub that never contacts the API. C optimizes for less code but gives up the reuse that motivated the
  request.
